  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a detailed explanation of why nitrogen is used in plastic welding:\n\n---\n\n## Why Nitrogen is Used in Plastic Welding\n\n### 1. \ud83d\udee1\ufe0f It Acts as a Shielding Gas\nNitrogen gas is used when plastic welding because it acts as a shielding gas, protecting the weld area from contaminants in the air. Much like shielding gases used when welding steel or aluminum with a MIG welder, it prevents the plastic from oxidizing during the welding process.\n\n---\n\n### 2. \ud83d\udd25 It Prevents Oxidation and Burning\nAir is composed of 80% nitrogen and 20% oxygen. The oxygen in the air causes oxidation, burning the plastic during the welding process. This results in smoke, ash, and sparks, which weaken the weld and create contamination.\n\nWelding with nitrogen eliminates this issue by flushing out the oxygen from the weld zone. Without oxygen, the plastic melts cleanly without burning or oxidizing. This allows the melted plastics to fuse together with no contamination for a stronger, more durable weld.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \ud83d\udcaa It Produces a Stronger, Cleaner Weld\nA nitrogen hot air welder uses compressed nitrogen gas to eliminate oxygen from the weld area. The nitrogen acts as a shielding gas and allows for a contaminant-free weld with less smoke, which creates a stronger weld.\n\nThe nitrogen shielding gas prevents oxidation, ensuring the strongest welds possible. This means you get a solid, long-lasting weld that stands up to vibration, cold, and heat.\n\n---\n\n### 4. \u2744\ufe0f It Helps with Thermal Management\nNitrogen also has thermodynamic properties that make it useful when welding plastics like ABS and polypropylene; its high heat capacity allows for faster cooling of the welded material without causing warping or cracking due to thermal shock.\n\n---\n\n### 5. \u267b\ufe0f It Is Used Efficiently\nThis type of welder can also switch to compressed air so that when preheating or cooling down the heating element, it does not waste the nitrogen.\n\n---\n\n### In Summary\nNitrogen is essentially an **inert gas** in this context \u2014 it doesn't react with the plastic the way oxygen does. By replacing oxygen at the weld site, it prevents burning, reduces smoke, and allows the plastic to melt and fuse cleanly, resulting in a much stronger and more reliable repair than hot air welding alone. Industry experts will tell you that you get a stronger and more durable repair using nitrogen plastic welders.",
